  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "This was requested by multiple users as a more flexible way to allow LDAP authentication.  For details, see:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "http://sourceforge.net/mailarchive/message.php?msg_id=46083EEA.2040004%40georgetown.edu"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": "http://sourceforge.net/mailarchive/message.php?msg_id=46083EEA.2040004%40georgetown.edu",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Hello all,",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "This is my first post to the list, so by way of introduction:",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "At Georgetown University, we are just beginning to do a Fedora pilot",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "project, so we're newbies in that arena. But we do have extensive",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "experience in various aspects of AAA middleware, including LDAP. I and",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "another Georgetown staff member are also on the Shibboleth core",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "development team (he is in fact the Shibboleth Java project lead). Over",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "the next few months we might be interested in contributing to Fedora in",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "those areas (via expertise and/or code) if there is a need and a desire",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "for that.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "I had previously sent some questions over on the users list regarding",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "the new LDAP auth filter that shipped with v2.2. I understand from",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Richard Green that there was a patch to the 2.2 version, documented here:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "http://www.fedora.info/wiki/index.php/Authenticating_Fedora_2.2_against_LDAP"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": "http://www.fedora.info/wiki/index.php/Authenticating_Fedora_2.2_against_LDAP",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The patch however has subsequently been found to have other bugs and so",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "the LDAP code is currently being rewritten. That being the case, I",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "wanted to offer a comment/suggestion on how the LDAP auth is configured",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "and attempted. Apologies if this has already been discussed.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "I haven't seen the patch, so I can only infer what the patch might be",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "doing based on the LDAP auth wiki topic, but the main observation that I",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "have is that: the original code does not support (and the patch does not",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "appear to support) the standard \"search-and-bind\" style of doing LDAP",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "auth. The style apparently supported in the patch by that new",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "init-param 'bind-filter' (which I would point out isn't really a filter,",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "BTW...) using a DN substitution pattern works for many people in many",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "environments (it does in fact work for our current LDAP environment here",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "at GU), but it makes 2 critical assumptions that don't hold for everyone:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "1) Assumes that users exist in a single OU/container in the directory -",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Many people have LDAP designs which put users in multiple",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "OU's/containers. You could mitigate that by having instead a list of DN",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "substitution patterns to try, but that's a little inefficient.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "2) Assumes that the \"username\" is part of the LDAP DN - This isn't",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "always the case. Some people use a unique, opaque identifier to",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "construct the DN, based on a GUID or a Person Registry ID or something",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "else that's unique and persistent. Others use other attributes of the",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "user to construct the last RDN component in the DN, for example Active",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Directory by default does something like 'CN=\"Brent Putman\", CN=Users,",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "DC=domain, DC=org' (or at least used to, I'm not an MS expert).",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "So the search-and-bind style of LDAP auth is the the only technique",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "(that I know of at least) that works for all environments and LDAP",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "designs. This is supported in and AFAIK is in fact the primary way that",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "people do LDAP auth in well-known software projects such as Apache",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "httpd, Apache Tomcat (via the JNDI Realm, although that supports both",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "bind styles) and most others that I've had occasion to work with. If",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "that gives you the warm fuzzies. ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "id": "1f642",
        "shortName": ":slight_smile:",
        "text": "🙂"
       },
       "type": "emoji"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "In short, that style of auth is:",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "1) bind to the directory, either anonymously or with a privileged DN",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "(depending on whether the users to be authenticated are publicly visible",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "and searchable within the directory)",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "2) search for the user by constructing an LDAP search filter based on",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "the supplied username, e.g. \"(uid=putmanb)\".",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "3) test user credentials by attempting to bind/rebind as the DN returned",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "in step 2, using the password supplied by the user",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "So since the code is being rewritten anyway, I would ask that these",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "comments be taken into account - it would make Fedora's LDAP",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "capabilities available to a broader array of organizations. Time",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "permitting (we're trying to get Shibboleth 2.0 out the door...), I might",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "be able to help in getting this implemented and/or tested , etc.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Thanks,",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Brent",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
